   },
   {
    "code": "SAMPLE_SIZE_POWER",
    "text": "Analyzing specific 'play X' and its sequential effects on opponent next-possession PPP and own-sequence lags will likely require a large number of instances for each play type to achieve sufficient statistical power, especially when breaking down by specific matchups or contexts. Rare play types or infrequent sequences might lead to insufficient sample sizes, making it difficult to detect meaningful effects or generalize findings."
   },
   {
    "code": "EXPOSURE_SPARSITY",
    "text": "The 'play X' mentioned in the question could refer to specific, less common play types, or specific sequences of play. If these instances are rare, or if the combinations of play X with subsequent opponent possessions or own-sequence lags are sparse, the data may not provide enough observations to reliably estimate PPP values and their statistical significance."
   }
  ]
 },
 "prior_work": {
  "already_answered": "partly",
  "cited": [
   "HYP:SF-APPS-060",
   "HYP:SF-APPS-073",
   "EXP:APPS-037",
   "HYP:SF07"
